    {
      "topLeftX": 263,
      "topLeftY": 122,
      "bottomRightX": 368,
      "bottomRightY": 169,
      "content": "$$\\eta = \\begin{bmatrix} \\cos (\\rho/2) \\\\ \\sin (\\rho/2) \\end{bmatrix}.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 178,
      "bottomRightX": 553,
      "bottomRightY": 212,
      "content": "Since $A$ was assumed to be a projection matrix, $\\eta$ is also the eigenvector of $A$ belonging to the eigenvalue $+1$. Thus,",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 244,
      "topLeftY": 220,
      "bottomRightX": 387,
      "bottomRightY": 297,
      "content": "$$\\begin{aligned} \\langle A\\psi, \\psi \\rangle &= \\langle\\langle\\psi, \\eta\\rangle\\eta, \\psi\\rangle \\\\ &= |\\langle\\psi, \\eta\\rangle|^2 \\\\ &= \\cos^2 (\\rho/2). \\end{aligned}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 90,
      "topLeftY": 305,
      "bottomRightX": 505,
      "bottomRightY": 323,
      "content": "Our problem is thus reduced to solving for $u(\\theta)$ the integral equation",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 227,
      "topLeftY": 334,
      "bottomRightX": 405,
      "bottomRightY": 368,
      "content": "$$\\cos^2 (\\rho/2) = \\int_{S^*} f_A(p)u(\\theta) \\, dp.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 376,
      "bottomRightX": 113,
      "bottomRightY": 392,
      "content": "Since",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 241,
      "topLeftY": 398,
      "bottomRightX": 386,
      "bottomRightY": 445,
      "content": "$$f_A(p) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{on } S_{P_{\\epsilon(A)}}^+ \\\\ 0 & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 452,
      "bottomRightX": 213,
      "bottomRightY": 469,
      "content": "this equation becomes",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 244,
      "topLeftY": 479,
      "bottomRightX": 386,
      "bottomRightY": 513,
      "content": "$$\\cos^2 (\\rho/2) = \\int_T u(\\theta) \\, dp$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 519,
      "bottomRightX": 280,
      "bottomRightY": 538,
      "content": "where $T = S_{P_{\\epsilon(A)}}^+ \\cap S_{P_\\theta}^+$. Thus,",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 195,
      "topLeftY": 544,
      "bottomRightX": 437,
      "bottomRightY": 580,
      "content": "$$\\cos^2 (\\rho/2) = \\int_{\\rho-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\int_{-\\varphi_\\theta}^{\\varphi_\\theta} u(\\theta) \\sin \\theta \\, d\\varphi \\, d\\theta$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 76,
      "topLeftY": 588,
      "bottomRightX": 553,
      "bottomRightY": 642,
      "content": "where $\\varphi_\\theta$ is the azimuthal angle of the point $Q = (\\sin \\theta \\cos \\varphi_\\theta, \\sin \\theta \\sin \\varphi_\\theta, \\cos \\theta)$ with polar angle $\\theta$ which lies on the great circle $C$ perpendicular to the point $P_{\\epsilon(A)} = (\\sin \\rho, 0, \\cos \\rho)$.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
